WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.402
Theo has just joined AMG and holds a Maryland producer license.

00:00:04.402 --> 00:00:10.006
Under agency law, the insurer is the principal and the producer is its agent.

00:00:10.006 --> 00:00:13.608
The producer acts on the insurer’s behalf, and the

00:00:13.608 --> 00:00:17.210
insurer is responsible for acts within the producer’s authority.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.011
A captive producer represents a single insurer.

00:00:20.011 --> 00:00:22.012
An independent producer represents several.

00:00:22.012 --> 00:00:25.214
Maryland uses one license, insurance producer, for people

00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:28.016
who historically were called agents or brokers.

00:00:28.016 --> 00:00:32.018
Whatever the label, when a producer sells an insurer’s policy,

00:00:32.018 --> 00:00:36.020
the insurer is the party whose authority is being exercised.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.795
The agency contract between Theo’s agency and the insurer spells out what he

00:00:40.795 --> 00:00:45.569
may do: solicit applications, quote approved rates, collect initial premiums and deliver policies.

00:00:45.569 --> 00:00:47.773
That written grant is express authority.

00:00:47.773 --> 00:00:52.180
It may also list what he may not do, such as alter

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:56.220
a policy or accept a risk the underwriters have not approved.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.893
A producer who acts within express authority binds the insurer.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:04.300
A producer who exceeds it may still bind the insurer in some

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.340
cases, which is why the next two kinds of authority exist.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.640
The contract cannot list every act.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.091
Implied authority covers what the public reasonably assumes a

00:01:14.091 --> 00:01:17.542
producer may do to carry out the express duties.

00:01:17.542 --> 00:01:20.992
If Theo may collect initial premiums, he impliedly may

00:01:20.992 --> 00:01:24.060
give a receipt and explain how coverage begins.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.044
If he may deliver a policy, he may answer ordinary questions about it.

00:01:29.044 --> 00:01:31.728
Implied authority comes from the position itself.

00:01:31.728 --> 00:01:36.329
It is still real authority, so the insurer is bound by it.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:39.779
On a question, if an act is a normal,

00:01:39.779 --> 00:01:43.230
necessary step toward an express task, call it implied.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:47.420
Apparent authority arises from what the insurer lets the public see.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:52.753
Suppose the insurer gives Theo business cards, application forms and a desk in an

00:01:52.753 --> 00:01:57.705
office with its logo, then quietly revokes his authority but tells no one.

00:01:57.705 --> 00:02:01.515
Rosa, an applicant, reasonably believes he still represents the company.

00:02:01.515 --> 00:02:06.848
Because the insurer created that appearance, it may be bound by what Theo does.

00:02:06.848 --> 00:02:10.276
Apparent authority protects innocent third parties; it never protects

00:02:10.276 --> 00:02:13.323
a producer who knows he has no authority.

00:02:13.323 --> 00:02:15.228
The key word is reasonable.

00:02:15.228 --> 00:02:20.180
The appearance must come from the insurer’s conduct, not the producer’s claims alone.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.656
Because the producer is the insurer’s agent, what the

00:02:23.656 --> 00:02:26.746
producer knows the insurer is treated as knowing.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:31.766
If Rosa tells Theo about a back injury and he leaves it off

00:02:31.766 --> 00:02:36.401
the application, the insurer may not later claim it was never informed.

00:02:36.401 --> 00:02:39.877
The producer also owes duties to applicants and insureds.

00:02:39.877 --> 00:02:44.511
He must handle premiums as a fiduciary, describe coverage accurately, disclose what

00:02:44.511 --> 00:02:49.146
the policy does not do, submit applications promptly and deliver the policy.

00:02:49.146 --> 00:02:53.008
These duties come from license law and from the general

00:02:53.008 --> 00:02:56.870
law of agency, and breaking them can cost the license.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.240
Here is the method.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.267
Find the act.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:03.034
Ask whether a written contract allows it; that is express authority.

00:03:03.034 --> 00:03:07.827
Ask whether it is a normal step toward an allowed act; that is implied.

00:03:07.827 --> 00:03:11.252
Ask whether the insurer’s own conduct led a reasonable applicant

00:03:11.252 --> 00:03:14.333
to believe the act was authorized; that is apparent.

00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:17.758
Then ask who is harmed if the act is disowned.

00:03:17.758 --> 00:03:21.524
Agency law generally protects the applicant who relied in good faith

00:03:21.524 --> 00:03:25.291
and leaves the insurer to recover from a producer who overstepped.

00:03:25.291 --> 00:03:29.400
Keep the producer’s separate duties to the client in view as well.
